{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789240044,"nonce":"goDlepq874R6hM0ZLmAfj1FRvf4=","title":"A function returning a growable list hands back a copy of its header","message":"Cranelift returns a struct through a caller-provided destination, copying\nthe bytes out of the callee's frame. A growable list's value is the\naddress of its heap header, and the header is the list's identity: a\nfunction that returns a list it did not build (an unbox, a pass-through)\nreturns a copy of the header, so the caller pushes into a header nobody\nelse can see and the original's length never changes.\n\nReproducer (Python frontend): a module-level list appended to inside a\nfunction and printed outside prints `[]`.\n\nFix: `destination_return_type` excludes the `{data, len, capacity}` list\nheader, which travels by address like a pointer. A list literal returned\ndirectly is heap-allocated for the same reason (`expected_growable` is\nset while translating a return whose declared type is a list). The LLVM\nbackend has not been checked for the same copy.","files":null}]}